# Patient global identity and new-patient-only KTP governance

Sprint doc: `docs/sprints/governance-patient-ktp-new-patient-only-1.md`.
Regression suite: `tests/Feature/RME/PatientKtpNewPatientOnlyGovernanceTest.php`
(declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`, selected by
the `PatientKtp` token) and `tests/js/ktp-camera-ocr-token.test.mjs`; 16/16
mutants killed when written.

These are **owner product decisions** (recorded 2026-10-11), not engineering
preferences. They extend — and relax none of — rule 101 (global patient identity
lookup), rule 172 (patient duplicate resolution / merge), rules 177–181 (KTP OCR,
supervised pilot, field read, D7 consent, live overlay), rule 182 (KTP archive
persistence) and rule 183 (KTP archive branch isolation). Where this rule and an
older note disagree about what is *required* of a patient, this rule wins.

The audit that produced it (2026-10-11) found the server side compliant and ONE
client-side violation of rule 13: the OCR attestation box was a native
`required`, so applying OCR in the "Pasien Baru" panel and then switching to
"Pasien Terdaftar" silently refused the existing-patient visit. That was fixed
in the same sprint (classification C); everything else was already compliant.

## Patient identity is global

1. **Master Data Pasien is global.** It is intentionally not branch-scoped.
2. Global patient **identity lookup and visibility** is an intentional clinical
   requirement, not by itself a security defect — do not branch-scope it in a
   security review. This does NOT dismiss real findings about what a
   cross-branch operator can read or change (e.g. the typed NIK on the patient
   edit form, cross-branch edits of master data); those stay legitimate
   findings and need an owner decision.
3. Do **not** add branch filtering to global patient lookup, canonical patient
   identity, patient search or the patient master-data repositories without an
   explicit, separate owner approval. Existing role/permission checks still apply.
4. The **same patient identity is reused across branches.** A patient registered
   at branch A who attends branch B is selected, not recreated (rule 101: the
   visit branch comes from the operator's work context, never from the patient).
5. Never create a branch-specific duplicate patient — not on a cross-branch
   visit, not to "own" a record at a branch, not to attach a document.

## KTP photo capture is a NEW-patient registration capability

6. KTP camera capture exists for **new patient registration** — exactly two
   surfaces: Master Data → Tambah Pasien, and Kunjungan → Daftar Kunjungan Baru →
   Pasien Baru (the partial `settings.patients._ktp-scan` is included only there).
   It is never offered on an existing patient's page.
7. Camera OCR is **optional** and stays behind its existing gates
   (`KtpCameraOcrPilotGate`, rule 178; D7 consent, rule 180). No code release
   widens or re-arms the pilot.
8. Scanner-agent capture and manual KTP file upload remain available where they
   are authorized today. No new capture permission is granted by this rule.
9. New patient registration keeps the **manual workflow** — typed data entry with
   no photo at all is a complete registration.
10. A missing KTP photo **never blocks registration.** `ktp_scan_token` is
    `nullable` on both registration requests, and no `rules()` method of any
    Request under `app/` may make a KTP-keyed field `required*` or `present`
    (pinned by a source scan that reads every entry to its top-level comma, so
    pipe strings and `Rule::requiredIf(...)` are seen). Only the two capture
    endpoints (`StoreKtpScanRequest`, `ParseKtpOcrRequest`) are excluded — they
    run only when a capture actually happens. The scan does not see rules added
    in `withValidator()`/`after()` or ad-hoc `Validator::make()`; never add a
    KTP requirement there either.

## Existing, repeat-visit, legacy and cross-branch patients

11. **Existing patients are not required** to upload or recapture a KTP photo.
12. **Legacy-imported patients** (`mst_patients.import_batch_id IS NOT NULL`) are
    not required to upload or recapture a KTP photo, and receive normal care.
13. **Repeat visits** require no KTP scan and no OCR. KTP state left behind in
    the hidden "Pasien Baru" panel never refuses an existing-patient visit — on
    the server (`StoreClinicVisitRequest` excludes the attestation unless the
    patient is new) **and in the browser**: no KTP control is ever a native
    `required`, because browsers validate `display:none` controls too and the
    refusal is silent (a console-only "not focusable"). Client-side KTP checks
    live in the `ktp-camera-ocr.js` submit guard, which skips a hidden section.
    This was a real defect found and fixed in this sprint (the OCR attestation
    box).
14. A **cross-branch visit** requires no KTP recapture.
15. **No mass legacy KTP backfill**, no KTP upload campaign, no KTP-photo
    completion popup, no KTP-photo/archive completion score, no KTP-related
    visit blocker. (The Sprint 61.0 audit's "KTP" column is the typed NIK
    number, not the photo — see the distinction under rule 16.)
16. The **absence of a KTP archive does not mean the patient identity is
    invalid** or the record incomplete. A patient's classification comes from
    workflow context (new-patient registration vs existing selection) and import
    provenance — never from whether a KTP image exists. Never classify a legacy
    patient as "new" for lacking a native visit, nor an existing patient as "new"
    for lacking a KTP image. No `patient_type` column exists or is needed.

   Distinction worth keeping: the Sprint 61.0 data-quality audit
   (`PatientDataCompletenessService`) scores the **typed NIK number**
   (`ktp_number`) as an *optional* field. That is the identity number, not the
   KTP image; it is informational, blocks nothing, and never reads the document
   archive (pinned). Changing whether the typed number counts is a separate
   owner decision.

## Existing archives and branch isolation

17. **Existing valid KTP archives are preserved** — never deleted, detached,
    rewritten, re-homed or copied to another branch to implement a policy.
18. KTP archive images remain **branch-isolated** (rule 183).
19. **Global patient search does not grant global KTP image access.** Seeing a
    patient everywhere and reading their card everywhere are different rights.
20. `PatientDocumentAccessService::decide()` remains the single KTP
    authorization source of truth; a refusal stays a 404 identical to a missing
    document; Super Admin and an explicit per-account grant remain the only
    cross-branch readers; Supervisor RME reads nothing without that grant.
21. `mst_patient_documents.captured_branch_id` is **server-owned and immutable**
    under current governance. It survives a cross-branch visit and a patient
    merge (the merge re-points `patient_id` only, via the query builder — the
    model's write-once guard does NOT protect that path; the regression test
    does). Never hand-edit it.

## Consent D7

22. D7 consent (`D7-2026-10-10`, rule 180) remains mandatory **where camera/OCR
    processing is actually used**. Its wording changes only through a new owner
    approval and a new version.
23. **Declining D7 never removes the manual pathway** — a declined photo is a
    plain upload with zero OCR requests, and typed registration is complete.
24. **Never fabricate consent**, and never treat a legacy or pre-existing
    patient as having consented to OCR. D7 does not by itself authorize
    indefinite document retention; the clinic privacy notice and retention
    policy remain owner-PENDING (rule 180) — never write them on the clinic's
    behalf.

## Permissions, storage, privacy

25. No automatic permission grant for reading old KTP documents. Cross-branch
    reading stays a direct, audited, per-account grant (rule 183).
26. No silent reassignment of patient document ownership.
27. KTP images stay on **private storage**, served only through the policy-gated
    stream with `Cache-Control: private, no-store`; never a public URL, never a
    `temporaryUrl()`.
28. A full NIK never appears in logs, audit payloads, reports, evidence or test
    fixtures (fictional NIKs only in tests).
29. **PatientMerge preserves the canonical global identity**: one person → one
    canonical patient; documents move with the patient and keep their capture
    branch.

## Change discipline

30. Any change to patient registration, visit registration, patient merge or the
    KTP archive must re-run the new-, existing-, legacy- and cross-branch-patient
    cases (this rule's regression suite plus rules 101/182/183 suites).
31. Deployment scripts (`scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh`) execute **on the VPS**,
    never on a workstation. A governance/docs/test-only change is closed without
    a deploy; never redeploy an unchanged runtime to satisfy a checklist.
32. A GO tag requires verified evidence. A docs/rules/tests-only GO tag names an
    undeployed commit and must say so; it never implies production runs it.
33. Cleanup leaves zero task-owned processes, worktrees, CI runs, locks or
    scratch files; never delete unrelated work, backups, evidence or patient data.

## What this rule does NOT authorize

- No attach-KTP-to-existing-patient feature is planned or required (rule 182's
  "known gap" is reframed by rule 11: it is not a gap in a requirement). If one
  is ever built it must be strictly optional, never prompted, never scored, never
  a visit precondition, and must keep rules 17–21.
- No branch-scoping of patient master data (an explicit owner decision would be
  needed; rule 3).
- No widening of the KTP camera pilot, no new consent records, no retention
  period.
